This paper investigates the standardized ground state solution for the given biharmonic Choquard type equation \begin{align*}
  \begin{split}
  \left\{
  \begin{array}{ll}
  \Delta^2u-\beta\Delta u=\lambda u+\left(\frac{1}{|x|^\mu}*F(u)\right)f(u),
  \quad\mbox{in}\ \ \mathbb{R}^4,
  \displaystyle\int_{\mathbb{R}^4}|u|^2dx=c^2,\quad u\in H^2(\mathbb{R}^4),
  \end{array}
  \right.
  \end{split}
  \end{align*} under specific constraints and growth conditions for $f$ and $F$, and establishes a key standardization result for a related variational problem. We further demonstrate that any standardized ground state solution satisfies a concise integral equation, and establish a connection to a minimization problem involving a constrained variational functional. Moreover, we prove the existence of a standardized ground state solution through a combination of mountain pass theorem and compactness of the embedding of Sobolev space. The key complication arises from the critical growth of the nonlinear term, which necessitates a nontrivial standardization of the variational functional, and requires a new approach to establish the required compactness of the embedding of Sobolev space. The technical novelty of this result mainly lies in handling the complex interplay between the biharmonic and nonlinear terms within the given equation. Additionally, we provide a new and innovative method for dealing with a specific type of variable coefficient term. The results obtained in this paper are novel and contribute significantly to the existing research on nonlinear Choquard type equations. Furthermore, the techniques developed can be applied to a broader class of nonlinear equations, and thus this work lays a new foundation for the study of other complex variational problems. The novel approach presented in this paper can also be viewed as an extension to the recent work appearing in [20-22, 24]. The complications arising in the analysis of the given equation, specifically the critical growth of the nonlinear term, can be viewed as a new development in the field, and the results obtained provide a significant contribution to the existing body of research. The results of this study can be seen as an extension to the work appearing in [20-22, 24], and the techniques developed can be applied to a broader class of

